WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.082
One request.

00:00:01.082 --> 00:00:03.612
A whole Tetris game in under three seconds.

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:07.801
Its makers wrote an engine for exactly one model, DeepSeek V four

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:10.022
point one Flash, in Rust and CUDA.

00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:11.609
What does that speed measure?

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:15.096
Hi, I'm Torque.

00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:18.472
Who does this engine serve, how does one step run on four

00:00:18.472 --> 00:00:20.442
GPUs, and where does the time go?

00:00:22.017 --> 00:00:24.703
Picture sixty-four users sending requests at once.

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.982
This server decodes one of them.

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:27.910
The rest wait.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.935
So seventeen forty is what one user sees, and all the box makes.

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:37.365
Each token picks six experts out of three hundred and eighty-four.

00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:41.937
A throughput engine sends each token to the GPU that owns its expert.

00:00:41.937 --> 00:00:45.769
At batch one, some GPUs get double work and one gets none.

00:00:46.189 --> 00:00:48.739
This engine slices every expert by its width.

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:52.883
Each GPU computes a quarter of all six, so the load is even.

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:57.436
Then the partial sums are added with plain stores over NV Link.

00:00:57.436 --> 00:00:58.469
No collective library.

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.298
Each step is one graph launch.

00:01:02.298 --> 00:01:04.840
The CPU only reads back the step before.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.868
Three draft layers guess six tokens at once.

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:11.154
The checkpoint says five; the engine raises it to six.

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:14.987
A confidence head picks how many guesses to check, and a switch

00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:16.703
inside the graph runs that body.

00:01:17.103 --> 00:01:19.311
All forty layers check the guesses together.

00:01:19.711 --> 00:01:22.368
On Tetris it keeps five point six tokens a step.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.580
On four GPUs a step takes three point three milliseconds.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.308
Double the GPUs and it only drops to two point seven one.

00:01:31.728 --> 00:01:35.560
Fit a line through both, and about two point one milliseconds never

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.753
shrinks: forty layers of small stages waiting on each other.

00:01:39.173 --> 00:01:44.208
Even with endless GPUs, that caps one stream near twenty-six hundred tokens a second.

00:01:45.575 --> 00:01:49.359
An outside model, from a team proposing its own chip, found the

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.881
same split: about half streaming, the rest waiting.

00:01:53.092 --> 00:01:57.126
Per GPU it makes four hundred and thirty-five tokens a second.

00:01:57.126 --> 00:02:00.059
Throughput engines make thirty to sixty times more.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:02.862
One request at a time.

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:06.311
One model, on Blackwell only.

00:02:07.207 --> 00:02:08.494
Temperature fixed at one.

00:02:09.916 --> 00:02:11.953
And a licence that is not open source.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.458
Good code, oversold numbers.

00:02:15.458 --> 00:02:19.530
One stream on this model is now limited by waiting, not bandwidth.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.227
The next win is there.

00:02:22.525 --> 00:02:26.714
One request, sliced experts, and a fixed cost GPUs cannot buy back.

00:02:26.714 --> 00:02:27.761
Thanks for watching!

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.239
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:30.827
I'm Torque.

00:02:30.827 --> 00:02:31.121
Bye!
